1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,344
This is a short update on the Google Cloud port of my multi-agent travel system.

2
00:00:04,344 --> 00:00:06,680
Everything you will see is running locally.

3
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:12,653
The Gemini model and the Google Cloud deployment are the next step, and I will say clearly where that line is.

4
00:00:13,853 --> 00:00:16,412
The orchestrator is Java with Spring A I.

5
00:00:16,412 --> 00:00:22,465
It delegates over A2A to a flight agent and a hotel agent, which use MCP servers for their tools.

6
00:00:22,465 --> 00:00:28,020
In this port, the flight agent is rewritten on Google's Agent Development Kit, in Python.

7
00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:34,011
The orchestrator, the MCP servers and the hotel agent are the existing Java services, unchanged.

8
00:00:34,011 --> 00:00:36,070
That is the point of A2A and MCP:

9
00:00:36,070 --> 00:00:39,440
the protocols stay stable while the framework changes.

10
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:44,578
Here the Java orchestrator asks for the status of its agents.

11
00:00:44,578 --> 00:00:47,741
It reads each agent's card with the Java A2A SDK.

12
00:00:47,741 --> 00:00:50,516
The flight agent is the new Python service.

13
00:00:50,516 --> 00:00:55,487
It is available, speaks protocol zero point three, and advertises two skills:

14
00:00:55,487 --> 00:00:57,681
flight search, and a policy check.

15
00:00:57,681 --> 00:01:03,426
In deployment that check reads BigQuery; locally it reads the same data from C S V files.

16
00:01:04,626 --> 00:01:14,193
The card declares a bearer security scheme, with the same name the Java agents use, so the existing orchestrator authenticates against the new agent without any change.

17
00:01:15,393 --> 00:01:16,892
Now auth and guardrails.

18
00:01:16,892 --> 00:01:17,453
No token:

19
00:01:17,453 --> 00:01:18,764
four hundred and one.

20
00:01:18,764 --> 00:01:19,513
Wrong token:

21
00:01:19,513 --> 00:01:20,824
four hundred and one.

22
00:01:20,824 --> 00:01:24,382
With the right token, I send a prompt injection over A2A.

23
00:01:24,382 --> 00:01:30,250
The guardrail blocks it before any model is called, and the task completes with a clear error.

24
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:32,560
No model was involved in that answer.

25
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:41,827
The integration tests run the real agent loop against the live Java MCP server, with a scripted stand-in for the model, so there is no model cost.

26
00:01:41,827 --> 00:01:49,507
They check that a search goes through authenticated MCP and arrives wrapped as untrusted data, with the booking tool hidden from the model.

27
00:01:49,507 --> 00:01:52,822
That an injection never reaches the model or the MCP server.

28
00:01:52,822 --> 00:01:57,851
And that with a wrong service token the agent fails closed, instead of improvising answers.

29
00:01:57,851 --> 00:01:59,950
The whole suite is thirty eight tests.

30
00:02:01,150 --> 00:02:03,042
The infrastructure is Terraform:

31
00:02:03,042 --> 00:02:13,506
a Cloud Run service with its own service account, a BigQuery dataset where the agent has read only access, and invoker bindings so that only the orchestrator can call the agent.

32
00:02:13,506 --> 00:02:18,413
It validates, but I have not applied it, because that needs a Google Cloud project.

33
00:02:19,613 --> 00:02:20,881
What is not done yet:

34
00:02:20,881 --> 00:02:30,789
running with Gemini on Vertex A I, deploying to Cloud Run and then Agent Engine, loading the BigQuery tables, and testing the OIDC authentication on real Cloud Run.

35
00:02:30,789 --> 00:02:34,353
That needs a Google Cloud project, and it is the next step.

36
00:02:34,353 --> 00:02:37,193
The narration voice in this video is synthetic.

